ok in another addition to the file that gets the most updates; i think ive figured out how to make BOTH the tile data and tile map "thread-safe"? for lack of a better term. so the plan is that there will be 2 tile data buffers overlapping in vram by spliting the bitdepth between them, two tile map "frames" in the BG map and a "canvas" in wram where tile data is composed before being carved into tiles. the rendering pipeline should look something like this;

the tile data for the frame will be drawn uncompressed into the canvas with a bit depth of 1, tile data will be generated based on this and kept probably nearby.
the tile data will be written to vram, however both the a and b buffers exist in the same area of memmory, with the a buffer being the even addresses and the b buffer being the the odd address.
the time map for the a buffer will be the top left 18x12 tiles and the tile map of the b buffer will be in the corisponding area directualy under it. 
when after an unknown amount of time all the data nessary for the frame is loaded into vram two things happen to switch which buffer is being rendered.
	firstly the BG pallet is changed between an 0100 pallet and an 0010 pallet where 0 is the desired "background" colour and 1 is the desireed "line" colour this is what selects which part of the overlapping tile data to use
	secondly the SCY regester is moved to the other tile map area, selecting the tile map data that is to be used

the idea behind this is that it compleatly avoids race conditions and allows graphics to be rendered at dynamic sub-refresh rate frame rates,

im going to rebuild the pixel drawer now that i have a working version of the line plotter, im going to base this off the way the NES version handles its tile data, the Wram graphics buffer will have;
	a byte array of 240 called tileList, a subset of this starting from the lower address side and ending with an $FF terminator will describe the tiles on the screen with pixles drawn to them
		the entries in this array will contain the "position" the tile should go on the screen which will be the linear index of the 18x12 area of the BG map that the 3D viewport will see these will be
		implicitly matched to the tiles data in their ordering so array[N] will hold the position that tile N of the wram buffer should go on the BG map,
		so the BG map value at position array[N] will be set to N+1
	a tile (8 Bytes) array tileData of 240 which will simply be the tile data, this array will get coppied as is into the tile data area in vram, this will have a special blank tile in position 0

psudo code for the PixelDrawer;
	DrawPixel(byte x, byte y){

	tileX = x >> 3
	tileY = y >> 3
	tilePos = tileX + 20(tileY)

	for (byte b in tileList){
		if (b = tilePos) { break }
		if (b = $ff){ break }
		freeTile ++
	}

	tileList[freeTile] = tilePos
	tileList[freeTile + 1] = $FF

	pixX = x AND &00000111
	pixY = y AND &00000111
	pixX = maskGen(pixX)
	pixOffset = 2((tilePos*8) + pixY)
	
	bit 0, pixOffset
	jr z,  :+
	pixOffset = pixOffset - 15
	:
	
	tileData[pixOffset] = tileData[pixOffset] XOR pixX

	}
	
this leaves a small problem, bitdepth, this generates a buffer of tiles and tile locations for 1-bitdepth 8x8 tiles but the PPU is expecting 2-bit depth 8x8 tiles, meaning that either this buffer would
	have to double in size with half of it being essentualy padding, or some method of getting the PPU to use the lower bit depth tile data is needed, if we continue to leverage CGB features we can use
	the BG attrutubte map, alogh with rotations of tiles this gives us access to tile spesific colour palets, this lets us hald the tile data storage and transfer size at the expence of multiplying the
	tile map transfer work by about 1.5 (doubling every second entry). given that the tile data is 16 times the size of the tile map data under the padding method, this seems like a worthwile tradeoff.
for every second tile entry the same tile list will have to be used , tilePos AND b11111110 will have to be written to the tile map instead and for the odd numbered entries the BG attribute map will
	have to be updated to use the alternate pallet. while the pallet switching was previously intended to handle the buffering of the tile data in vram, it will now be used to facalitate (hopefully) the
	full transfer of an entire viewport of tiles in a single Vblank using 1980 bytes of the 2280 bytes of possable Vblank DMA throughput, with the tile map population (hopefully) fitting in the
	remaining time. this is the worse case scanario where the entire viewport of tiles must be changed out between frames, if this works it means the engine could render at a full 60 FPS, if the
	graphics buffer can be populated in time, this may require near constant double speed opperation but would be very impressive if successful (if i do say so myself)
ok the snippet of assembly added to the psudo code along with doubling the address offset beforehand should produce the propper interleaving of the tiles data such that the the pallet buffering methods 
	above should be able to selectivly render the indivdual tiles from each pair as based on the BG attrutubte map entries, tiles with non-odd (this means 0 is included) entries on the tileList will
	use their own tile id and the normal pallet while the odd numbers will use the tile ID of the entry before and the alternate pallet
